WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.600
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.190
Today we're going to talk about a procedural
algorithm. But first of all, what does procedural

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:16.150
mean? Procedural graphics is an exciting subfield
of computer graphics where instead of storing

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:22.910
a lot of stuff, information is generated on
the fly. For instance, in photorealistic rendering,

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:28.010
we're trying to simulate how digital objects
would look like in real life. We usually seek

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:34.390
to involve some scratches on our digital models,
and perhaps add some pieces of bump or dirt

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:39.960
on the surface of the model. To obtain this,
we can just ask the computer to not only generate

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:45.320
them on the fly, but we can also edit them
as we desire. We can also generate cloudy

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:50.980
skies and many other things where only some
statistical properties have to be satisfied,

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:55.949
like how many clouds we wish to see and how
puffy they should be, which would otherwise

00:00:55.949 --> 00:01:01.229
be too laborious to draw by hand. We are scholars
after all, we don't have time for that.

00:01:01.229 --> 00:01:05.580
There are also computer games where the levels
we can play through are not predetermined,

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:11.730
but also generated on the fly according to
some given logical constraints. This can mean

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:16.840
that a labyrinth should be solvable, or the
level shouldn't contain too many enemies that

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:22.840
would be impossible to defeat. The main selling
point is that such a computer game has potentially

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.020
an infinite amount of levels.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:30.660
In this paper, a technique is proposed to
automatically generate procedural yarn geometry.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:36.010
A yarn is a piece of thread from which we
can sew garments. The authors extensively

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:42.280
studied parameters in physical pieces of yarns
such as twisting and hairyness and tried to

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.640
match them with a procedural technique.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.130
So, for instance, if in a sudden trepidation
we wish to obtain a realistic looking piece

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:56.980
of cotton, rayon or silk in our light simulation
programs, we can easily get a unique sample

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:02.360
of a chosen material, which will be very close
to the real deal in terms of these intuitive

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:08.269
parameters like hairyness. And we can not
only get as long or as many of these as we

00:02:08.269 --> 00:02:12.459
desire, but can also edit them according to
our artistic vision.

00:02:12.459 --> 00:02:18.519
The solutions are validated against photographs
and even CT scans. I always emphasize that

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:23.659
I really like these papers where the solutions
have some connection to real world around

00:02:23.659 --> 00:02:26.409
us. This one is super fun indeed!

00:02:26.409 --> 00:02:33.180
The paper is a majestic combination of beautifully
written mathematics and amazing looking results.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:34.790
Make sure to have a look!

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:39.389
And you know, we always hear these news where
other YouTubers have problems with what is

00:02:39.389 --> 00:02:44.290
going on in their comments section. Well,
not here with our Fellow Scholars. Have a

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:50.459
look at the comments section of our previous
episode. Just absolutely beautiful. I don't

00:02:50.459 --> 00:02:56.749
even know what to say, it feels like a secret
hideout of respectful and scholarly conversations.

00:02:56.749 --> 00:03:02.590
It's really amazing that we are building a
community of Fellow Scholars, humble people

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:05.409
who wish nothing else than to learn more.

00:03:05.409 --> 00:03:09.549
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

